# Browser verification before human review

A pull request that changes what a person sees or does in a browser must be exercised in a browser before a human reviews it. CI decides whether a PR needs that and writes the decision in one "Browser verification" comment. Until a passing run is published, reviewer requests on such a PR are taken back.

This file is the playbook for the AI coding agent that does the run. The person starts it with `<%= command %> NUMBER` for an open PR, or `<%= command %>` on a branch that has no PR yet.

## Steps

1. Run `browser-review-gate status` (add `--pr NUMBER` when a number was given). It prints JSON; follow `next`:
   - `nothing`: no run is needed, or the PR is already verified. Say that review can be requested.
   - `check_out_pr_head`: check out the PR head with a clean worktree, then start again.
   - `request_assessment`: run `browser-review-gate request-assessment NUMBER`, wait about a minute, then start again.
   - `publish_saved`: a run saved for this commit exists. Run `browser-review-gate publish` and stop; nothing runs again.
   - `open_pull_request`: a run is saved and waits for the PR. Tell the person to open it.
   - `ask_to_start_app`: the app is not running. Do not start it yourself: ask the person to start it (`app.start_command` says how) and stop until they have.
   - `run_and_publish` or `run_and_save`: continue with step 2.
2. Decide what to run.
   - `scenarios` in the status output is the list CI wrote for this PR. Run every one of them, and give each case the `id` of its scenario. You may add cases of your own; you may not drop or rename one from the list.
   - With no PR yet, or an empty list, choose the scenarios yourself: read the diff against the base branch, then `<%= rules_path %>`, which says what this project always checks and which scenarios each area needs. Its rules are mandatory. CI compares your cases with its own list once the PR is open and asks for what is missing.
   - Do not re-run behavior an earlier passing case still covers.
3. Open the running app in a browser tool. `app` in the status output gives the URL and a sign-in hint. Never start, restart or stop the app yourself: what runs on the person's machine is their decision. Use local test data and do not destroy existing data. If the app, the data, or a browser tool is missing, report the blocker. Reading code and running unit tests does not replace a browser run.
4. Run every scenario end to end. After key actions check the page state, console errors, and failed requests. For each case record the page it was exercised on (`url`) and what you observed there, including console errors and failed requests (`details`). A case without both is refused. A skipped or blocked scenario is not a pass.
5. Write the report as JSON outside the repository (see the shape below). Set `coverage_complete` to true only when every scenario from step 2 ran. CI checks the report against its list by case `id`: a missing scenario takes the label away again.
6. Publish or save, from the checkout that was tested, with everything committed and pushed:
   - PR exists: `browser-review-gate publish --report PATH` (add `--pr NUMBER` when the branch has several). It posts the cases with their outcomes. Only a complete all-pass report adds the `<%= label %>` label.
   - No PR yet: `browser-review-gate save --report PATH`. The run is published when the PR for this commit is opened. A new commit makes it stale.
7. Check the command result and the PR. A non-zero exit can still mean a report was posted: a failing or incomplete report is visible in the PR and review stays closed.
8. Request a reviewer only when the person named one and only after the run passed. Reviewers whose requests CI took back earlier are asked again automatically. Otherwise say that review can be requested.

## When a scenario cannot be run

Some scenarios cannot be exercised locally, for example a sign-in through an external provider. Do not mark the run complete and do not drop the scenario silently. Tell the person. They can list it under `excluded` in the report, or someone other than the author can put the `<%= waiver_label %>` label on the PR to let review proceed without a run.

## Report shape

```json
{
  "tested_sha": "<full commit SHA that was tested>",
  "coverage_complete": true,
  "cases": [
    { "id": "BROWSER-1", "name": "The changed interaction completes", "result": "pass", "url": "/the/page/exercised", "details": "What was observed, with console errors and failed requests if any." }
  ],
  "excluded": [
    { "name": "Sign-in through an external provider", "reason": "Needs a real provider account; covered by integration tests." }
  ]
}
```

`result` is `pass` or `fail`. `url` is an http(s) URL or a path starting with `/`. `excluded` is optional: list a scenario there only when the person decided it stays outside the local run. Never exclude a scenario on your own to make the run complete. Keep customer and real company names out of the report.

## Rules

- Run the awkward path in the browser, or mark coverage incomplete and leave review closed. Do not declare a run done from tests and code reading.
- Keep the report file and browser artifacts outside the repository. The publisher refuses a checkout with modified or untracked files and lists them.
- After an unrelated commit, do not run everything again: use the PR comment and the earlier report to pick only what is new.
